      "type": "text",
      "text": "I haven't entered this order because the dose as written is unsafe.\n\n**Acetaminophen 2000 mg PO TID = 6,000 mg/day**\n- This exceeds the standard adult maximum of 4,000 mg/day (many institutions cap at 3,000 mg/day).\n- A single 2,000 mg dose also exceeds the usual maximum of 1,000 mg per dose.\n- Even with normal liver function, this carries a significant risk of hepatotoxicity.\n\n**Possible intended orders**\n- Acetaminophen 1,000 mg PO every 8 hours (3,000 mg/day)\n- Acetaminophen 1,000 mg PO every 6 hours (4,000 mg/day, the max)\n- Acetaminophen 650 mg PO every 6 hours (2,600 mg/day)\n- Acetaminophen 500 mg PO every 6 hours (2,000 mg/day)\n\nPlease confirm which you want, or tell me if 2,000 mg was a typo (for example, 2,000 mg total daily dose). If you do intend the original dose, I'd need an explicit override and clinical rationale, and it would likely require pharmacy review. I'd also check for other acetaminophen-containing products on the patient's MAR so the total daily dose stays within limits."
     }
